# Regular differentials on X₀(N)_ℚ̄ are weight-two cusp forms

Theorem statement:
`ModularCurve.exists_cuspForm_coeffMap_diffQExpBar_eq_qExpansion_of_mem_regularDifferentialsBar`
(`1fa447ab-933b-5209-83b9-b9eca9a63e48`), by Claude, 2026-09-05.

Proof: submission `58538eff-0347-5df8-bfc7-7e524e1fd05e`, by Claude, 2026-09-05 (ACCEPTED); locally
adapted.

Fix a nonzero natural number $N$ and a ring homomorphism $\iota_0 : \overline{\mathbb Q} \to \mathbb
C$ out of `AlgebraicClosure ℚ`. Let $\overline F_N$ denote `modularFunctionFieldBar N`, the
intermediate field of $\overline{\mathbb Q}((q))$ over $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ generated by the
coefficientwise image of `modularFunctionFieldFull N` (itself the subfield of $\mathbb Q((q))$
generated over $\mathbb Q$ by the set `divisorExpansions N`). Let $\omega$ be an element of the
module of Kähler differentials $\Omega[\overline F_N / \overline{\mathbb Q}]$, and assume $\omega$
lies in `regularDifferentialsBar N`, i.e. for every place $v$ of $\overline F_N$ over
$\overline{\mathbb Q}$ there is an element $g$ of the valuation subring of $v$ with $\omega = g
\cdot v.\mathrm{dCoord}$. The conclusion asserts the existence of a cusp form $f$ of weight $2$ for
`CongruenceSubgroup.Gamma0 N` such that the Laurent series $\mathrm{diffQExpBar}\,N\,\omega \in
\overline{\mathbb Q}((q))$ — the image of $\omega$ under the $\overline F_N$-linear map obtained
from the derivation `qEulerOn` ($q\,d/dq$) by the universal property of Kähler differentials —
becomes, after applying $\iota_0$ to each coefficient, exactly the image in $\mathbb C((q))$ of the
power series `UpperHalfPlane.qExpansion 1 f`, the $q$-expansion of $f$ at the cusp $\infty$ with
width $1$.

This is the $\Omega \to S_2$ half of the $q$-expansion dictionary between global regular
differentials on $X_0(N)$ over $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ and weight-two cusp forms on $\Gamma_0(N)$, in
the direction producing a cusp form from a differential. It is used in the proof of the genus bound
`CuspForm.genusFormula_le_finrank_gamma0_weight_two`
and in the construction of the linear equivalence
`ModularCurve.
exists_linearEquiv_kaehlerH0_baseChange_intLattice_of_ratCurveModel_of_cuspSection_compat_of_neZero`
between the differentials of a rational model and the integral lattice of cusp forms.

